scenario_questions.scenario:
You are adding discounts to a console-based order calculator. Current implementation hard-codes rules:

```csharp
public decimal CalculatePrice(Order order)
{
    decimal price = order.Lines.Sum(l => l.UnitPrice * l.Qty);
    if (order.Customer.IsVip) price *= 0.90m;
    if (price > 1000m) price *= 0.95m;
    return price;
}
```
New requirements:
- Seasonal discount (10%) applies only to non-VIP customers.
- First-time buyer discount (15%) cannot be combined with any other discount.
- Business wants to add/remove rules without editing `CalculatePrice` and to control their application order.
You must keep it as a console app for now, but make it testable.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
A good answer introduces a strategy-like abstraction for discount rules with metadata for priority and combinability, composes them externally, and applies them in order with LINQ. For example:

```csharp
public interface IDiscountRule
{
    int Priority { get; }
    bool CanCombine { get; }
    bool IsApplicable(Order order);
    decimal Apply(decimal currentPrice, Order order);
}

public sealed class VipRule : IDiscountRule
{
    public int Priority => 100; // lower runs earlier
    public bool CanCombine => true;
    public bool IsApplicable(Order o) => o.Customer.IsVip;
    public decimal Apply(decimal p, Order o) => p * 0.90m;
}

public sealed class HighValueRule : IDiscountRule
{
    public int Priority => 200;
    public bool CanCombine => true;
    public bool IsApplicable(Order o) => p(o) > 1000m;
    decimal p(Order o) => o.Lines.Sum(l => l.UnitPrice * l.Qty);
    public decimal Apply(decimal p, Order o) => p * 0.95m;
}

public sealed class SeasonalNonVipRule : IDiscountRule
{
    public int Priority => 150;
    public bool CanCombine => true;
    public bool IsApplicable(Order o) => !o.Customer.IsVip && o.SeasonalActive;
    public decimal Apply(decimal p, Order o) => p * 0.90m;
}

public sealed class FirstTimeBuyerRule : IDiscountRule
{
    public int Priority => 50;
    public bool CanCombine => false; // cannot combine with any other
    public bool IsApplicable(Order o) => o.Customer.IsFirstTime;
    public decimal Apply(decimal p, Order o) => p * 0.85m;
}

public class PricingEngine
{
    private readonly IReadOnlyList<IDiscountRule> _rules;
    public PricingEngine(IEnumerable<IDiscountRule> rules) =>
        _rules = rules.OrderBy(r => r.Priority).ToList();

    public decimal CalculatePrice(Order order)
    {
        decimal basePrice = order.Lines.Sum(l => l.UnitPrice * l.Qty);
        var applicable = _rules.Where(r => r.IsApplicable(order)).ToList();
        // If any non-combinable rule applies, pick the highest-priority one only
        var nonComb = applicable.FirstOrDefault(r => !r.CanCombine);
        if (nonComb != null) return nonComb.Apply(basePrice, order);
        // Otherwise apply all combinable in priority order
        return applicable.Aggregate(basePrice, (p, r) => r.Apply(p, order));
    }
}
```
Justification: Each rule is isolated, easily unit-testable, and can be added/removed via composition (e.g., DI or a factory in Program.cs). Priority enforces order deterministically. The non-combinable case is enforced by branching: if any such rule applies, it short-circuits others. The engine exposes pure logic without I/O, so unit tests can assert pricing outcomes for different orders. Console UI remains a thin shell invoking `PricingEngine`.

scenario_questions.scenario:
A console tool must process thousands of lines from files using asynchronous I/O. A teammate wrote:

```csharp
var lines = System.IO.File.ReadAllLines(path);
Parallel.ForEach(lines, async line =>
{
    await ProcessAsync(line); // writes to an async sink
});
Console.WriteLine("Done");
```
`Done` prints before all lines are processed, and sometimes some work is lost under load.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Diagnosis: `Parallel.ForEach` expects a synchronous body and does not understand `async` lambdas. The `async` lambda here is an `async void` delegate under the hood, fire-and-forget, so `Parallel.ForEach` completes without awaiting. Work can still be in-flight or dropped if exceptions occur.

Fix: Use `Task`-aware composition. Options include `Task.WhenAll` over a projection, `Parallel.ForEachAsync` (in .NET 6+) with proper `ValueTask` handling, and/or a `SemaphoreSlim` to bound concurrency.

Example with `WhenAll` and bound concurrency:

```csharp
var throttler = new SemaphoreSlim(8);
var tasks = File.ReadLines(path)
    .Select(async line =>
    {
        await throttler.WaitAsync();
        try { await ProcessAsync(line); }
        finally { throttler.Release(); }
    });
await Task.WhenAll(tasks);
Console.WriteLine("Done");
```

Or with `Parallel.ForEachAsync`:

```csharp
await Parallel.ForEachAsync(File.ReadLines(path), new ParallelOptions { MaxDegreeOfParallelism = 8 },
    async (line, ct) => await ProcessAsync(line));
Console.WriteLine("Done");
```
Why it works: Both versions return a `Task` you can await, ensuring completion before printing `Done`. Concurrency is explicitly bounded. Exceptions propagate via the awaited `Task` instead of being lost.

scenario_questions.scenario:
A flaky unit test occasionally fails in CI:

```csharp
public class TokenGenerator
{
    private static readonly Random _rng = new Random();
    public string NextToken() => _rng.Next(0, 1000000).ToString();
}

[Fact]
public void GeneratesUniqueTokens()
{
    var gen = new TokenGenerator();
    var tokens = Enumerable.Range(0, 10000).Select(_ => gen.NextToken());
    Assert.Equal(10000, tokens.Distinct().Count());
}
```
Failures are rare but happen.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Source: `Random` is not cryptographically strong and can produce collisions; using a shared static `Random` also introduces thread-safety issues and test coupling. The uniqueness assertion over 10k draws from ~1e6 space is probabilistic and will flake.

Redesign for testability: Inject a number/string source so tests can control it, and in production use a stronger generator if uniqueness is required (e.g., `Guid` or `RandomNumberGenerator`). Tests should verify behavior, not probabilistic uniqueness.

Example:

```csharp
public interface ITokenSource { string Next(); }
public sealed class GuidTokenSource : ITokenSource
{
    public string Next() => Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");
}

public sealed class SequenceTokenSource : ITokenSource
{
    private int _i;
    public string Next() => (_i++).ToString();
}

public class TokenGenerator
{
    private readonly ITokenSource _source;
    public TokenGenerator(ITokenSource source) => _source = source;
    public string NextToken() => _source.Next();
}

// Test
[Fact]
public void GeneratesIncreasingTokens_WithSequenceSource()
{
    var gen = new TokenGenerator(new SequenceTokenSource());
    var tokens = Enumerable.Range(0, 5).Select(_ => gen.NextToken()).ToList();
    Assert.Equal(new[]{"0","1","2","3","4"}, tokens);
}
```
In production `Program` composes `TokenGenerator` with `GuidTokenSource`. Deterministic tests assert documented behavior (format/order), not statistical properties.

scenario_questions.scenario:
A console app aggregates people by identity using a `HashSet<Person>` and `Distinct()`:

```csharp
public class Person
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public override bool Equals(object obj) => ((Person)obj).Id == Id;
    public override int GetHashCode() => 42; // placeholder
}

var people = new[]{ new Person{Id=1,Name="A"}, new Person{Id=1,Name="A"}, new Person{Id=2,Name="B"} };
var distinct = people.Distinct().ToList(); // sometimes behaves poorly in larger sets
```
`Distinct()` appears slow and sometimes misses expected de-duplication in a larger dataset when `Id` changes during processing.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Problems: `Equals` lacks null/type checks and will throw on non-`Person`. `GetHashCode` returning a constant causes extreme hash collisions and poor performance. Using a mutable property (`Id`) in equality leads to undefined behavior in `HashSet`/`Distinct` if it changes while in a set.

Fix 1 (override properly, keep identity immutable):

```csharp
public class Person
{
    public int Id { get; }
    public string Name { get; set; }

    public Person(int id, string name) { Id = id; Name = name; }

    public override bool Equals(object obj)
        => obj is Person other && other.Id == Id;

    public override int GetHashCode() => Id.GetHashCode();
}
```
Now identity is immutable and hashing stable; `Distinct()` and `HashSet<Person>` behave correctly and fast.

Fix 2 (leave class mutable, supply comparer at use site):

```csharp
sealed class PersonIdComparer : IEqualityComparer<Person>
{
    public bool Equals(Person x, Person y) => x?.Id == y?.Id;
    public int GetHashCode(Person obj) => obj?.Id.GetHashCode() ?? 0;
}

var distinct = people.Distinct(new PersonIdComparer()).ToList();
```
Trade-offs: Overriding ties equality to the type globally, which is appropriate for true identity. A custom comparer localizes equality and is safer if `Id` can change or multiple notions of equality are needed. In all cases, avoid mutating fields that participate in hashing while the object is in a hashed collection.

