scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Use a CSS-only stacked-card pattern at small widths, preserving semantic table structure for desktop and screen readers. Approach:

- Keep table markup for desktop. At narrow viewports, switch `display` of `table`, `thead`, `tbody`, `tr`, `td` to block so each row becomes a card. Use `data-label` attributes to show headers for each cell, improving readability.
- Hide the visual `<thead>` on small screens but keep it for accessibility via `aria-hidden`.
- Ensure cells wrap and break long words using `word-break` and allow responsive spacing.

Example CSS:

```css
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .bookings { border: 0; }
  .bookings thead { position: absolute; left: -9999px; height: 1px; width: 1px; overflow: hidden; }
  .bookings tr { display: block; border: 1px solid #ddd; margin-bottom: 12px; }
  .bookings td { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 8ch 1fr; gap: 8px; padding: 8px 12px; }
  .bookings td::before { content: attr(data-label); font-weight: 600; color: #555; }
  .bookings td { word-break: break-word; }
}
```
Optional small markup change: ensure each `td` has `data-label` matching its header text (already present). For accessibility, keep native table semantics; the `<thead>` remains in the DOM for screen readers even if visually hidden. This avoids horizontal scrolling, presents a clear label-value stack on mobile, and maintains the correct reading order. Desktop view remains an actual table with no changes outside the media query.

