})
.AddCookie("Cookies", o => { /* ... */ })
.AddJwtBearer("Bearer", o => { /* ... */ });
```

Now `[Authorize]` on API endpoints with a Bearer header authenticate via JWT; browser requests without that header use Cookies. Admin MVC actions continue to work.

2) Alternatively, split endpoints and set defaults per endpoint group:

- Keep `DefaultScheme = "Cookies"` globally for MVC.
- For APIs, set:

```csharp
services.AddAuthorization(options =>
{
    options.FallbackPolicy = new AuthorizationPolicyBuilder()
        .AddAuthenticationSchemes("Bearer")
        .RequireAuthenticatedUser()
        .Build();
});
```

…and mark API controllers with `[ApiController]` and place them under `/api` route. Then map API endpoints with a convention applying `RequireAuthorization()` and `RequireAuthorization(new AuthorizeAttribute { AuthenticationSchemes = "Bearer" })` if using minimal routing groups. In MVC, use a custom `AuthorizeFilter` added via `AddControllersWithViews` to set Cookies.

In both approaches, `[Authorize]` requires no per-action scheme parameter. At runtime, the policy scheme chooses JWT when the `Authorization` header is present, otherwise cookies. This avoids regressions by centralizing the rule and covering new endpoints automatically.

Make the GET-by-id routable by name and use `CreatedAtRoute` or `CreatedAtAction` in POST. Example:

```csharp
[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class AccountsController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet("{id}", Name = "GetAccount")]
    public ActionResult<AccountDto> Get(int id) => ...;

    [HttpPost]
    public async Task<IActionResult> Post(AccountCreateDto dto)
    {
        // `[ApiController]` automatically returns 400 if ModelState is invalid
        if (await _svc.EmailExistsAsync(dto.Email))
        {
            return Problem(
                statusCode: StatusCodes.Status409Conflict,
                title: "Email already exists",
                detail: $"An account with {dto.Email} already exists.");
        }

        var acc = await _svc.CreateAsync(dto);
        return CreatedAtRoute("GetAccount", new { id = acc.Id }, acc);
    }
}
```

- Success: `CreatedAtRoute("GetAccount", new { id }, acc)` produces `201` and the Location header.
- Duplicate: use `Problem(..., statusCode: 409)` for a consistent body.
- Validation: `[ApiController]` short-circuits to `400` with ProblemDetails before action executes; no manual `ModelState` checks needed.

Ensure the GET route has a `Name` or pass the correct action name to `CreatedAtAction(nameof(Get), ...)`.

scenario_questions.scenario:
A singleton caching service captures a scoped dependency and occasionally throws `ObjectDisposedException` under load.

```csharp
public interface ICurrentUser { string UserId { get; } }
public class HttpContextCurrentUser : ICurrentUser
{
    private readonly IHttpContextAccessor _ctx;
    public HttpContextCurrentUser(IHttpContextAccessor ctx) => _ctx = ctx;
    public string UserId => _ctx.HttpContext?.User?.FindFirst("sub")?.Value;
}

public class CatalogCache
{
    private readonly ICurrentUser _user; // captured at singleton construction
    public CatalogCache(ICurrentUser user) { _user = user; }
    public string KeyPrefix() => _user.UserId; // sometimes throws
}

services.AddHttpContextAccessor();
services.AddScoped<ICurrentUser, HttpContextCurrentUser>();
services.AddSingleton<CatalogCache>();
```

The cache is injected into controllers and background services.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
The singleton `CatalogCache` depends on scoped `ICurrentUser`, which is invalid. The singleton is created once and holds a reference to a scoped object whose lifetime is per-request. Under concurrency, it can see a disposed `HttpContext` or the wrong user.

Fix by removing the captured scoped dependency and deferring resolution per operation via a factory or `IHttpContextAccessor` read at call time. Options:

1) Inject `IServiceProvider` and create a scope per call:

```csharp
public class CatalogCache
{
    private readonly IServiceProvider _sp;
    public CatalogCache(IServiceProvider sp) => _sp = sp;
    public string KeyPrefix()
    {
        using var scope = _sp.CreateScope();
        var user = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ICurrentUser>();
        return user.UserId;
    }
}
```

2) Better for ASP.NET requests: inject `IHttpContextAccessor` (singleton-safe) and compute on demand without holding onto a scoped service:

```csharp
public class CatalogCache
{
    private readonly IHttpContextAccessor _http;
    public CatalogCache(IHttpContextAccessor http) => _http = http;
    public string KeyPrefix() => _http.HttpContext?.User?.FindFirst("sub")?.Value;
}
```

Register:

```csharp
services.AddHttpContextAccessor();
services.AddSingleton<CatalogCache>();
// remove scoped ICurrentUser from singleton's constructor
```

Or change `CatalogCache` to scoped if it truly must hold user-specific state. These designs avoid capturing a disposed scoped service and align lifetimes correctly.

