scenario_questions.scenario:
After deploying your React SPA to `/app/` behind Nginx, deep links like `https://example.com/app/orders/123` return a 404 on refresh. Static files are served from `/var/www/app`. API calls are proxied to `https://api.example.com` under `/api`. Current server block:

```nginx
location /app/ {
  root   /var/www;
  try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
}

location /api/ {
  proxy_pass https://api.example.com/;
}
```

Your React app also uses `import.meta.env.VITE_API_BASE` and builds assets with relative paths.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
The 404 happens because the server tries to resolve `/app/orders/123` as a real file and returns 404; the SPA router needs index.html for unknown paths. Also, assets might break if the base path is wrong. Fixes:
- Nginx: serve index.html as a fallback under `/app/` and still serve real files. For example:

```nginx
location /app/ {
  root /var/www;                 # /var/www/app/index.html exists
  try_files $uri $uri/ /app/index.html;
}
```

This returns `/var/www/app/index.html` for any unknown sub-route so the SPA can render `orders/123`.
- Ensure the client builds with the correct base/public path so asset URLs resolve under `/app/` (Vite example in `vite.config.ts`):

```typescript
export default defineConfig({
  base: '/app/',
});
```

- Keep API proxy distinct so `/api/*` does not hit the SPA fallback:

```nginx
location /api/ {
  proxy_pass https://api.example.com/;
  # include CORS headers if needed
}
```

- Set `VITE_API_BASE` at deploy time to `https://example.com/api` so the client calls the correct absolute base from the `/app/` subpath.

Justification: `try_files` with `/app/index.html` preserves static files while routing unknown paths to the SPA. Setting `base` prevents broken asset links (e.g., `/assets/...` vs `/app/assets/...`). Keeping `/api` in a separate location block avoids the SPA fallback catching API calls.

scenario_questions.scenario:
You built a controlled checkout form with async server-side validation. Current submit logic:

```tsx
async function onSubmit(e: React.FormEvent) {
  e.preventDefault();
  setSubmitting(true);
  setErrors({});
  const res = await fetch(`${api}/checkout`, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify(form)
  });
  const data = await res.json();
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(data.message || 'Unknown');
  setToast('Order placed');
}
```

The API returns 422 with JSON `{ errors: { email: 'Invalid', card: 'Declined' } }`. The UI only shows "Unknown" and sometimes flashes stale errors when users edit fields while a previous request is in flight.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Changes:
- Model per-field errors in state and render them next to inputs:

```typescript
type FieldErrors = Partial<Record<'email' | 'card' | 'name', string>>;
const [errors, setErrors] = useState<FieldErrors>({});
```

- Parse errors without assuming JSON is present only after checking `ok`. Branch on `res.ok` first; if false, read JSON safely and set field errors:

```typescript
const res = await fetch(...);
if (res.ok) {
  const data = await res.json();
  // success flow
  setToast('Order placed');
  return;
}
let payload: any = undefined;
try { payload = await res.json(); } catch {}
if (payload?.errors) setErrors(payload.errors as FieldErrors);
else setToast('Checkout failed');
```

- Prevent races with either AbortController or a submit token:

```typescript
const abortRef = useRef<AbortController | null>(null);
async function onSubmit(e: FormEvent) {
  e.preventDefault();
  abortRef.current?.abort();
  const ctrl = new AbortController();
  abortRef.current = ctrl;
  setSubmitting(true);
  setErrors({});
  const res = await fetch(url, { signal: ctrl.signal, ... });
  // handle as above
}
```

Alternatively, use a monotonically increasing `requestId` and ignore out-of-order responses.

- Disable the submit button when `submitting` to avoid parallel submits and use defensive rendering so errors clear on field change:

```typescript
onChange={e => { setForm(f => ({...f, email: e.target.value })); setErrors(err => ({...err, email: ''})); }}
```

Why this works: Parsing conditionally on `ok` prevents `json()` exceptions and maps server 422 to field-level messages. Aborting previous requests or ignoring stale responses removes stale error flashes from earlier submissions completing later.

scenario_questions.scenario:
CI runs React component tests with Jest + Testing Library. A component uses a loading spinner controlled by a `setTimeout` inside `useEffect` to delay showing an empty state by 500ms. Locally tests pass; in CI they are flaky. Snippets:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setTimeout(() => setShowEmpty(true), 500);
  return () => clearTimeout(id);
}, [items.length]);
```

Test:

```tsx
test('shows empty state after delay', async () => {
  render(<List items={[]} />);
  await waitFor(() => expect(screen.getByText(/no items/i)).toBeInTheDocument());
});
```

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Use modern fake timers to control the timeout deterministically and flush it immediately in the test:

```typescript
beforeEach(() => {
  jest.useFakeTimers({ legacyFakeTimers: false });
});

afterEach(() => {
  jest.useRealTimers();
});

test('shows empty state after delay', () => {
  render(<List items={[]} />);
  // no need to wait for real 500ms
  act(() => { jest.advanceTimersByTime(500); });
  expect(screen.getByText(/no items/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

Alternatively, inject the delay as a prop (default 500) and set it to 0 in tests:

```tsx
function List({ items, emptyDelay = 500 }: { items: string[]; emptyDelay?: number }) {
  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setTimeout(() => setShowEmpty(true), emptyDelay);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
  }, [items.length, emptyDelay]);
  // ...
}
```

Then in test `render(<List items={[]} emptyDelay={0} />)`; no timers needed.

Why it works: Fake timers or an injectable delay remove dependence on real time and CI scheduling variance, making assertions deterministic and instant.
